---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
---

<DocsLayout title="Quick Start">
  <h1>Quick Start</h1>
  <p>Run Identity locally with the embedded SQLite backend — no external services — then sign up a user and verify the access token.</p>

  <h2>1. Start Identity (SQLite, fastest start)</h2>
  <p>
    The quickest way to run is the embedded SQLite backend
    (<code>GATEWAY_REPO_DRIVER=sqlite</code>, pure-Go
    <code>modernc.org/sqlite</code>). It needs no external database — point
    <code>GATEWAY_SQLITE_PATH</code> at a file on a mounted volume so data
    survives restarts (use <code>:memory:</code> for a throwaway instance).
  </p>
  <Code code={`docker run -d --name identity \\
  -p 80:80 -p 9090:9090 \\
  -v identity-data:/data \\
  -e GATEWAY_REPO_DRIVER=sqlite \\
  -e GATEWAY_SQLITE_PATH=/data/identity.db \\
  -e GATEWAY_DEFAULT_PROJECT_ID=proj_local \\
  -e GATEWAY_PASSKEY_RP_ID=localhost \\
  -e GATEWAY_PASSKEY_ORIGIN=http://localhost \\
  -e GATEWAY_AUTH_ALLOW_LOCAL=true \\
  ghcr.io/elloloop/identity:1.2.0`} lang="bash" title="docker run (sqlite)" />
  <p>
    With no JWT key file configured, Identity auto-generates a
    throwaway RSA key (kid <code>dev</code>) on startup and logs
    <code>jwt_signer_dev_key_generated</code>.
    See <a href="/identity/docs/installation/jwt-keys">JWT Keys</a> for production setup.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Production: use Postgres</h2>
  <p>
    For multi-node or production deployments, run against Postgres
    (<code>GATEWAY_REPO_DRIVER=postgres</code>) instead. Start a Postgres
    and point Identity at it with a DSN:
  </p>
  <Code code={`docker run -d --name identity \\
  -p 80:80 -p 9090:9090 \\
  -e GATEWAY_REPO_DRIVER=postgres \\
  -e GATEWAY_POSTGRES_DSN=postgres://identity:secret@postgres:5432/identity \\
  -e GATEWAY_POSTGRES_AUTO_MIGRATE=true \\
  -e GATEWAY_DEFAULT_PROJECT_ID=proj_local \\
  -e GATEWAY_PROJECT_SECRETS_KEY="$(openssl rand -base64 32)" \\
  -e GATEWAY_PASSKEY_RP_ID=localhost \\
  -e GATEWAY_PASSKEY_ORIGIN=http://localhost \\
  -e GATEWAY_AUTH_ALLOW_LOCAL=true \\
  ghcr.io/elloloop/identity:1.2.0`} lang="bash" title="docker run (postgres)" />
  <p>
    <strong>Before you expose a fresh Postgres deployment to the internet,
    bootstrap the first platform admin</strong> — the one-time, trust-on-first-use
    <code>CreateFirstPlatformAdmin</code> RPC — over a private network. Otherwise
    an anonymous caller can win the first-admin race in the window before you
    create it. To harden the RPC itself, set <code>GATEWAY_ADMIN_API_SECRET</code>
    (which then also gates the bootstrap on the <code>X-Admin-Secret</code>
    header) or <code>GATEWAY_DISABLE_FIRST_ADMIN_BOOTSTRAP=true</code> to close it
    once the first admin exists. See
    <a href="/identity/docs/installation/docker">Docker</a> for the full flow.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Sign up a user</h2>
  <p>
    Connect-RPC accepts JSON over HTTP. Each RPC is a POST to
    <code>/identity.v1.IdentityService/&lt;Method&gt;</code> with the request
    message as the body and <code>Content-Type: application/json</code>.
  </p>
  <Code code={`curl -sX POST http://localhost/identity.v1.IdentityService/PasswordSignup \\
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\
  -d '{"email":"alice@example.com","password":"correct horse battery"}'`} lang="bash" />
  <p>Response (truncated):</p>
  <Code code={`{
  "user": {
    "id": "user_...",
    "email": "alice@example.com",
    "status": "USER_STATUS_ACTIVE",
    "role": "admin"
  },
  "accessToken": "eyJhbGciOiJSUzI1NiIs...",
  "refreshToken": "rt_...",
  "expiresIn": 900
}`} lang="json" />

  <h2>4. Call an authenticated RPC</h2>
  <Code code={`TOKEN=$(curl -sX POST http://localhost/identity.v1.IdentityService/PasswordLogin \\
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\
  -d '{"email":"alice@example.com","password":"correct horse battery"}' \\
  | jq -r .accessToken)

curl -sX POST http://localhost/identity.v1.IdentityService/GetCurrentUser \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \\
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\
  -d '{}'`} lang="bash" />

  <h2>5. Verify the token externally</h2>
  <p>
    Downstream services should fetch the JWKS and verify access tokens
    locally rather than calling Identity on every request.
  </p>
  <Code code={`curl -s http://localhost/.well-known/jwks.json`} lang="bash" />
  <Code code={`{
  "keys": [
    {"kty":"RSA","use":"sig","alg":"RS256","kid":"dev","n":"...","e":"AQAB"}
  ]
}`} lang="json" />

  <h2>6. Inspect metrics</h2>
  <Code code={`curl -s http://localhost:9090/metrics | head`} lang="bash" />

